\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Battery v5 (final analysis). Verdicts are statements about the \emph  {registered six-lag lookup class}. ``Detected'' means the parametric-bootstrap $p$ is at its floor ($<.004$); no series has $p$ between $.004$ and $.023$, so any level in that range gives the same verdicts. This is a conservative analysis rule adopted during the final analysis, not a preregistered significance level. The verdicts, applied in this order: ``history adds'' $=$ detected, with both moving-block lower bounds above the practical floor $0.03$ (a certificate is issued); ``detected (exploratory)'' $=$ detected, point gain above the floor, lower bounds below it; ``detected, below floor'' $=$ detected, point gain at or below the floor; ``equivalent'' $=$ not detected and $U_2<0.03$; ``not established'' $=$ not detected and $U_2\ge 0.03$; ``inconclusive'' $=$ the calibrations disagree (footnote). Detection takes precedence, so X-ray, which also has $U_2<0.03$, is reported as detected. ``Exploratory'' refers to the size of the gain, not to the calendar-block analysis. The i.i.d.\ null ($p=.023$) is the selection control. $L^{\mathrm  {blk}}_{D^*}$ and $L^{\mathrm  {blk}}_{\max }$ are the one-sided 95\% moving-block lower bounds at the pre-selected lag and with lag selection repeated inside each replicate (the latter bounds the \emph  {maximal} gain over the grid). $^{\ddagger }$Inconclusive: the calibrations disagree. The fitted-Markov calibration finds the estimator biased upward ($U_2<0$), while both moving-block lower bounds are positive and the selection-aware one ($+.040$) exceeds the practical floor, and the detection test does not fire on this short series ($p=.997$; $N=4{,}698$ pairs, the second-shortest, Table\nonbreakingspace \ref {tab:details}). We claim neither equivalence nor a certificate. The i.i.d.\ null's \emph  {positive} paired interval is the empirical lag-selection bias (its upper end, $\approx 0.02$, is the claim floor for paired bounds, since $D^*$ is an argmax the bootstrap treats as fixed). Bold: the one interval that dwarfs every calibration concern. Sample sizes, lag grids, selected lags and block lengths are in Table\nonbreakingspace \ref {tab:details}.}}{14}{table.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:battery}{{4}{14}{Battery v5 (final analysis). Verdicts are statements about the \emph {registered six-lag lookup class}. ``Detected'' means the parametric-bootstrap $p$ is at its floor ($<.004$); no series has $p$ between $.004$ and $.023$, so any level in that range gives the same verdicts. This is a conservative analysis rule adopted during the final analysis, not a preregistered significance level. The verdicts, applied in this order: ``history adds'' $=$ detected, with both moving-block lower bounds above the practical floor $0.03$ (a certificate is issued); ``detected (exploratory)'' $=$ detected, point gain above the floor, lower bounds below it; ``detected, below floor'' $=$ detected, point gain at or below the floor; ``equivalent'' $=$ not detected and $U_2<0.03$; ``not established'' $=$ not detected and $U_2\ge 0.03$; ``inconclusive'' $=$ the calibrations disagree (footnote). Detection takes precedence, so X-ray, which also has $U_2<0.03$, is reported as detected. ``Exploratory'' refers to the size of the gain, not to the calendar-block analysis. The i.i.d.\ null ($p=.023$) is the selection control. $L^{\mathrm {blk}}_{D^*}$ and $L^{\mathrm {blk}}_{\max }$ are the one-sided 95\% moving-block lower bounds at the pre-selected lag and with lag selection repeated inside each replicate (the latter bounds the \emph {maximal} gain over the grid). $^{\ddagger }$Inconclusive: the calibrations disagree. The fitted-Markov calibration finds the estimator biased upward ($U_2<0$), while both moving-block lower bounds are positive and the selection-aware one ($+.040$) exceeds the practical floor, and the detection test does not fire on this short series ($p=.997$; $N=4{,}698$ pairs, the second-shortest, Table~\ref {tab:details}). We claim neither equivalence nor a certificate. The i.i.d.\ null's \emph {positive} paired interval is the empirical lag-selection bias (its upper end, $\approx 0.02$, is the claim floor for paired bounds, since $D^*$ is an argmax the bootstrap treats as fixed). Bold: the one interval that dwarfs every calibration concern. Sample sizes, lag grids, selected lags and block lengths are in Table~\ref {tab:details}}{table.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Tightness of the information envelope on approximately balanced binary pairs (Proposition\nonbreakingspace \ref {prop:tight}). Points: per-lag ratio $\sqrt  {\hat  I/2}\,/\,\mathaccent "0362{V}_1$ of the information envelope to the exact single-sample value, both computed from the same pooled lag table, for the series with $|\hat  \pi -\tfrac  12|<.035$ (lags with $\mathaccent "0362{V}_1>.025$; the i.i.d.\ null has none). Theorem\nonbreakingspace \ref {thm:mi} applied to that table forces every point to at least one. The horizontal axis is $2\mathaccent "0362{V}_1$, which equals $|r|$ for a symmetric pair, so the curve is the symmetric limit $\sqrt  {I(r)/2}\,/\,(|r|/2)$ and the dashes its leading correction $1+r^2/12$. Points above the curve at small $\mathaccent "0362{V}_1$ are the mildly imbalanced series, whose single-sample value is pulled toward Theorem\nonbreakingspace \ref {thm:hinge}'s threshold (Fig.\nonbreakingspace \ref {fig:trichotomy}). A consistency check, not an independent test.}}{15}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tightness}{{2}{15}{Tightness of the information envelope on approximately balanced binary pairs (Proposition~\ref {prop:tight}). Points: per-lag ratio $\sqrt {\hat I/2}\,/\,\widehat V_1$ of the information envelope to the exact single-sample value, both computed from the same pooled lag table, for the series with $|\hat \pi -\tfrac 12|<.035$ (lags with $\widehat V_1>.025$; the i.i.d.\ null has none). Theorem~\ref {thm:mi} applied to that table forces every point to at least one. The horizontal axis is $2\widehat V_1$, which equals $|r|$ for a symmetric pair, so the curve is the symmetric limit $\sqrt {I(r)/2}\,/\,(|r|/2)$ and the dashes its leading correction $1+r^2/12$. Points above the curve at small $\widehat V_1$ are the mildly imbalanced series, whose single-sample value is pulled toward Theorem~\ref {thm:hinge}'s threshold (Fig.~\ref {fig:trichotomy}). A consistency check, not an independent test}{figure.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The battery at a glance. Dots: maximum history-class gain over the exact single-sample value; grey bars: the model-calibrated interval $[\hat  g-\varepsilon _{\mathrm  {over}},\hat  g+\varepsilon _{\mathrm  {under}}]$; ticks: the one-sided 95\% moving-block bounds ($B=2000$ replicates) at fixed $D^*$ (upper, teal) and with in-replicate lag selection (lower, orange); solid vertical line: zero; dotted vertical line: the registered practical floor $0.03$. Seismic and BGP separate from both zero and the floor; the i.i.d.\ null's positive selection-aware tick is the empirical winner's-curse floor; whale's wide bar is the short-series noise the verdict language respects. Where $\varepsilon _{\mathrm  {under}}<0$ (whale, i.i.d.) the bias-corrected upper endpoint $U_2$ sits \emph  {below} the point estimate, because the estimator is upward-biased there, and the bound says so.}}{16}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:battery}{{3}{16}{The battery at a glance. Dots: maximum history-class gain over the exact single-sample value; grey bars: the model-calibrated interval $[\hat g-\varepsilon _{\mathrm {over}},\hat g+\varepsilon _{\mathrm {under}}]$; ticks: the one-sided 95\% moving-block bounds ($B=2000$ replicates) at fixed $D^*$ (upper, teal) and with in-replicate lag selection (lower, orange); solid vertical line: zero; dotted vertical line: the registered practical floor $0.03$. Seismic and BGP separate from both zero and the floor; the i.i.d.\ null's positive selection-aware tick is the empirical winner's-curse floor; whale's wide bar is the short-series noise the verdict language respects. Where $\varepsilon _{\mathrm {under}}<0$ (whale, i.i.d.) the bias-corrected upper endpoint $U_2$ sits \emph {below} the point estimate, because the estimator is upward-biased there, and the bound says so}{figure.caption.9}{}}
